<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Saalfeld</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Saalfeld/Saale</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Saalfeld is a town on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saale">Saale</a> river in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thuringia">Thuringia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. An old mining and trading town, it was the residence of the dukes of Saxe-Saalfeld and then of Saxe-Coburg-Saalfeld until 1826, when it passed to Saxe-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meiningen">Meiningen</a>. On 10 October 1806 the Battle of Saalfeld, in which Prince Louis Ferdinand of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> was killed, opened <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>'s campaign against Prussia.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/saalfeld.jpg" alt="Saalfeld" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Michael Sander · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Stadtkirche_Saalfeld.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Saalfeld is the capital of the Saalfeld-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rudolstadt">Rudolstadt</a> district of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thuringia">Thuringia</a>. It lies in the valley of the Saale River north of the Thuringian Highland, 48 km south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weimar">Weimar</a>, and the local mountain is the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chelmno">Kulm</a> at 481.9 metres. Together with Rudolstadt and Bad Blankenburg it forms a tri-city area of about 70,000 people. It is a popular base for hikers on the Rennsteig, a ridge trail of 168 km, and was traditionally known for its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> mines.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Saalfeld was possibly founded by the 7th century around a fortress now called Hoher Schwarm or Sorbenburg, and it is first mentioned in a deed of 899. In 1012 Henry II ceded the former Carolingian Kaiserpfalz to Count Palatine Ezzo of Lotharingia, whose daughter Richeza left it to the Archbishops of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cologne">Cologne</a>. Archbishop Anno II founded a Benedictine abbey there in 1071, which was destroyed in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-peasants-war">German Peasants' War</a> of 1526. The citizens received town privileges in 1208 from the Counts of Schwarzburg, and in 1389 the town passed to Landgrave Balthasar of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thuringia">Thuringia</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-wettin">House of Wettin</a>. Mines, transport on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saale">Saale</a> and fishing, shown by the barbels in the coat of arms, made it prosper.</p><p>In 1675 Duke Albert V of Saxe-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coburg">Coburg</a> chose Saalfeld as his residence, and from 1677 Saalfeld Castle was built on the site of the destroyed abbey. His brother John Ernest IV called himself Duke of Saxe-Coburg-Saalfeld, and the castle was home to four generations of that line. Duke Francis's daughter Victoria became the mother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/queen-victoria">Queen Victoria</a>, and his son Ernest III was the father of Prince Albert. Another son, Leopold, became in 1831 the ancestor of the royal House of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a>. On 10 October 1806 a Prussian and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> force met a corps of the French army under Marshal Jean Lannes at the Battle of Saalfeld, where Prince Louis Ferdinand of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> was killed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>After the First World War Saalfeld became part of the new Free State of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thuringia">Thuringia</a>. As a railway junction it was strongly affected by bombing in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">World War II</a>, and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-germany">German Democratic Republic</a> its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steam-locomotive">steam locomotive</a> shed made it a destination for railway enthusiasts. The renovated castle is now the town administrative building. The Thüringer Symphoniker Saalfeld-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rudolstadt">Rudolstadt</a>, an orchestra formed in 1992 by a merger, gives concerts in both Saalfeld and Rudolstadt, and the town has about 28,000 inhabitants.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Saalfeld” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After two days of combat at Wattignies, a French army under Jean-Baptiste Jourdan defeated a Coalition army directed by Prince Josias of Saxe-Coburg-<b>Saalfeld</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/october-16">16 October in history</a></li><li><blockquote>In the Ernestine redistribution of 1826, Duke Ernest I of Saxe-Coburg-<b>Saalfeld</b> gave up Saalfeld and received Gotha, forming Saxe-Coburg and Gotha.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-saxe-coburg-and-gotha">Duchy of Saxe-Coburg and Gotha</a></li><li><blockquote>John Ernest, seventh son of Ernest the Pious, received <b>Saalfeld</b> in 1680.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-saxe-coburg-saalfeld">Duchy of Saxe-Coburg-Saalfeld</a></li><li><blockquote>On his death in 1675 his lands were divided among seven sons, creating the duchies of Coburg, Meiningen, Römhild, Eisenberg, Hildburghausen and <b>Saalfeld</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-saxe-gotha">Duchy of Saxe-Gotha</a></li><li><blockquote>The duchy later gained parts of Saxe-Römhild and, in the 1826 redistribution, Hildburghausen and <b>Saalfeld</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-saxe-meiningen">Duchy of Saxe-Meiningen</a></li><li><blockquote>His lands were divided among Saxe-Gotha-Altenburg, Saxe-Meiningen and Saxe-Coburg-<b>Saalfeld</b> after a long dispute, and Römhild later passed to Saxe-Meiningen.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-saxe-romhild">Duchy of Saxe-Römhild</a></li></ul></section>
